A "decision" is a choice the group deliberated and reached *during this meeting* — there was an open question or goal on the table, alternatives or trade-offs were genuinely weighed, and the group settled on one. Apply these rules strictly. Most meetings produce few or no decisions, so do not hunt for one; inventing a decision where the group never weighed options is worse than returning nothing. When in doubt, leave it out:
- The decision must be reached in this meeting. Exclude anything a speaker merely reports as already settled: pre-existing facts, standing policies, or how things already work (e.g. "our pricing is token-based, not per-user"), and choices made before or outside this meeting that are only being recounted here (e.g. "we decided last year to use a single account"). If the choice was already made when the meeting started, it is not a decision.
- A genuine decision resolves a question that was actually deliberated. If a course of action was simply agreed as the obvious next step — no goal framed, no alternatives considered — it is an action item, not a decision, even when no single person is named (e.g. "Convictional will build a custom demo for Aksel's team to share" is a committed next step, not a deliberated choice).
- Include a decision reached implicitly only when there is a concrete proposal AND clear affirmative agreement or consensus AND no unresolved pushback. If a thread trails off unresolved, exclude it.
- Include explicit decisions NOT to do something (e.g. "decided not to rebrand this year").
- Exclude action items and tasks. A decision records WHAT the group resolved; a task records WHO will do work next. Any "<person> will <do something>" or assignment of future work is an action item — leave it out (e.g. "Aksel will fly to Atlanta", "Marla will set up a call"). Tentative commitments to look into, explore, check, or see if something is possible are action items too (e.g. "Marla will explore importing the data on their behalf"), never decisions. A scope or approach choice is still a decision even when phrased with "will" (e.g. "we will import the existing goals before building status updates"); the test is whether a question was settled or an option chosen, not whether someone committed to a task.
- Also exclude: proposals or ideas that were not adopted, opinions and general discussion, unresolved debate, and deferrals or topics parked for later (e.g. "we'll revisit next week").
- Attribute each decision to the person who made or drove it; use "the group" when no single owner is clear. Never guess an owner.
